abstract:
A new relativistic formulation of MOND is advanced, involving two metrics as independent degrees of freedom: the MOND metric ${g}_{\ensuremath{\mu}\ensuremath{
u}}$, to which alone matter couples, and an auxiliary metric ${\stackrel{^}{g}}_{\ensuremath{\mu}\ensuremath{
u}}$. The main idea hinges on the fact that we can form tensors from the difference of the Levi-Civita connections of the two metrics, ${C}_{\ensuremath{ eta}\ensuremath{\gamma}}^{\ensuremath{\alpha}}={\ensuremath{\Gamma}}_{\ensuremath{ eta}\ensuremath{\gamma}}^{\ensuremath{\alpha}}\ensuremath{-}{\stackrel{^}{\ensuremath{\Gamma}}}_{\ensuremath{ eta}\ensuremath{\gamma}}^{\ensuremath{\alpha}}$, and these act like gravitational accelerations. In the context of MOND, we can form dimensionless ``acceleration'' scalars and functions thereof (containing only first derivatives) from contractions of ${a}_{0}^{\ensuremath{-}1}{C}_{\ensuremath{ eta}\ensuremath{\gamma}}^{\ensuremath{\alpha}}$. I look at a subclass of bimetric MOND theories governed by the action $I=\ensuremath{-}(16\ensuremath{\pi}G{)}^{\ensuremath{-}1}﻿\ensuremath{\int}[\ensuremath{ eta}{g}^{1/2}R+\ensuremath{\alpha}{\stackrel{^}{g}}^{1/2}\stackrel{^}{R}\ensuremath{-}2(g\stackrel{^}{g}{)}^{1/4}f(\ensuremath{\kappa}){a}_{0}^{2}\mathcal{M}(\stackrel{	exttildelow{}}{\ensuremath{\Upsilon}}/{a}_{0}^{2})]﻿{d}^{4}x+{I}_{M}({g}_{\ensuremath{\mu}\ensuremath{
u}},{\ensuremath{\psi}}_{i})+{\stackrel{^}{I}}_{M}({\stackrel{^}{g}}_{\ensuremath{\mu}\ensuremath{
u}},{\ensuremath{\chi}}_{i})$, with $\stackrel{	exttildelow{}}{\ensuremath{\Upsilon}}$ as a scalar quadratic in the ${C}_{\ensuremath{ eta}\ensuremath{\gamma}}^{\ensuremath{\alpha}}$, $\ensuremath{\kappa}=(g/\stackrel{^}{g}{)}^{1/4}$, ${I}_{M}$ as the matter action, and allow for the existence of twin matter that couples to ${\stackrel{^}{g}}_{\ensuremath{\mu}\ensuremath{
u}}$ alone. Thus, gravity is modified not by modifying the elasticity of the space-time in which matter lives, but by the interaction between that space-time and the auxiliary one. In particular, I concentrate on the interesting and simple choice $\stackrel{	exttildelow{}}{\ensuremath{\Upsilon}}\ensuremath{\propto}{g}^{\ensuremath{\mu}\ensuremath{
u}}({C}_{\ensuremath{\mu}\ensuremath{\lambda}}^{\ensuremath{\gamma}}{C}_{\ensuremath{
u}\ensuremath{\gamma}}^{\ensuremath{\lambda}}\ensuremath{-}{C}_{\ensuremath{\mu}\ensuremath{
u}}^{\ensuremath{\gamma}}{C}_{\ensuremath{\lambda}\ensuremath{\gamma}}^{\ensuremath{\lambda}})$. This theory introduces only one new constant, ${a}_{0}$; it tends simply to general relativity (GR) in the limit ${a}_{0}\ensuremath{ightarrow}0$ and to a phenomenologically valid MOND theory in the nonrelativistic limit. The theory naturally gives MOND and ``dark energy'' effects from the same term in the action, both controlled by the MOND constant ${a}_{0}$. In regards to gravitational lensing by nonrelativistic systems--a holy grail for relativistic MOND theories--the theory predicts that the same potential that controls massive-particle motion also dictates lensing in the same way as in GR: Lensing and massive-particle probing of galactic fields will require the same ``halo'' of dark matter to explain the departure of the present theory from GR. This last result can be modified with other choices of $\stackrel{	exttildelow{}}{\ensuremath{\Upsilon}}$, but lensing is still enhanced and MOND-like, with an effective logarithmic potential.

MOND has limelighted the fact that Newtonian dynamics and general relativity (GR) have not been verified to any accuracy at very low accelerations---at or below the MOND acceleration ${a}_{0}$: Without invoking made-to-measure ``dark matter,'' Newtonian dynamics (and hence general relativity) fail in accounting for galactic dynamics at such low accelerations. In particular, we do nont have evidence that all the cherished, underlying principles of Newtonian dynamics or GR, such as locality or Lorentz invariance, still apply in the MOND limit. I discuss the possibility that the principle of general covariance might not apply in this limit. This would be in line with suggestions that general covariance, where it does hold, is only an emergent, and hence approximate, property of relativistic dynamics. This idea also resonates well with MOND, which hinges on accelerations, for example, because the existence of an effective absolute inertial frame is natural in MOND. Relaxing general covariance affords more freedom in constructing candidate MOND theories. For example, it may permit constructing pure-metric, local MOND theories, which is thought impossible with general covariance. I exemplify this with a MOND-oriented, oversimplified, noncovariant theory, where the gravitational Lagrangian is ${\mathcal{L}}_{M}\ensuremath{\propto}{\ensuremath{\ell}}_{M}^{\ensuremath{-}2}\mathcal{F}({\ensuremath{\ell}}_{M}^{2}\mathcal{R})$, where $\mathcal{R}={g}^{\ensuremath{\mu}\ensuremath{
u}}({{\mathrm{\ensuremath{\Gamma}}}^{\ensuremath{\gamma}}}_{\ensuremath{\mu}\ensuremath{
u}}{{\mathrm{\ensuremath{\Gamma}}}^{\ensuremath{\lambda}}}_{\ensuremath{\lambda}\ensuremath{\gamma}}\ensuremath{-}{{\mathrm{\ensuremath{\Gamma}}}^{\ensuremath{\gamma}}}_{\ensuremath{\mu}\ensuremath{\lambda}}{{\mathrm{\ensuremath{\Gamma}}}^{\ensuremath{\lambda}}}_{\ensuremath{
u}\ensuremath{\gamma}})/2$ is the (nonscalar) first-derivative part of the Ricci scalar $R$. ${{\mathrm{\ensuremath{\Gamma}}}^{\ensuremath{\gamma}}}_{\ensuremath{\mu}\ensuremath{
u}}$ is the Levi-Civita connection of a metric ${g}_{\ensuremath{\mu}\ensuremath{
u}}$, which couples minimally to matter, and ${\ensuremath{\ell}}_{M}={c}^{2}/{a}_{0}$ is the MOND length, which is of cosmological magnitude, being, e.g., of the order of the de Sitter radius of our Universe. This ${\mathcal{L}}_{M}$ gives a covariant theory in the high-acceleration limit by requiring that $\mathcal{F}(z)\ensuremath{ightarrow}z+\ensuremath{\zeta}$, for $z\ensuremath{\gg}1$, which gives GR with a cosmological constant $\ensuremath{\zeta}{c}^{\ensuremath{-}4}{a}_{0}^{2}$. In the MOND limit, ${\mathcal{F}}^{\ensuremath{'}}(z\ensuremath{\ll}1)\ensuremath{\propto}{z}^{1/2}$. In the nonrelativistic limit, the metric has a solution of the form ${g}_{\ensuremath{\mu}\ensuremath{
u}}\ensuremath{\approx}{\ensuremath{\eta}}_{\ensuremath{\mu}\ensuremath{
u}}\ensuremath{-}2\ensuremath{\phi}{\ensuremath{\delta}}_{\ensuremath{\mu}\ensuremath{
u}}$, as in GR, but the potential $\ensuremath{\phi}$ solves a MOND, nonlinear Poisson analog. This form of ${g}_{\ensuremath{\mu}\ensuremath{
u}}$ also produces gravitational lensing as in GR, only with the MOND potential. I show that this theory is a fixed-gauge expression of bimetric MOND (BIMOND), with the auxiliary metric constrained to be flat. The latter theory is thus a covariantized version of the former \`a la St"uckelberg. This theory is also a special case of so-called $f(\mathcal{Q})$ theories---aquadratic generalizations of ``symmetric, teleparallel GR,'' which are, in turn, also equivalent to constrained BIMOND-type theories.
